.TH CBMAP_DELETE_ELEM 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cbmap_delete_elem \- remove a key-value pair from the BST map
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

ccol_retval_t cbmap_delete_elem(cbmap cbm, const cmap_pair *key_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Removes the key-value pair with the given key from the BST map, frees the
memory of the key and of the value, and then balances the tree again with
AVL rotations.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cbm        BST map to remove from
key_pair   Key to remove
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        - The function removed the key
ccol_key_not_found  - The key is not in the map
ccol_invalid_args   - key_pair is NULL, key_pair carries a NULL ptr
                      with a non-zero size, or the size of key_pair
                      does not match the size of a fixed-width key type
                      (see cbmap_create_full(3))
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no particular alignment, because the
comparison reads the key bytes with memcpy and never through a pointer
cast to the key type. A caller can therefore point key_pair->ptr straight
at key bytes that sit at an arbitrary offset inside a larger buffer, such
as a field sliced out of a packed network frame or a memory-mapped
record, without copying them into an aligned local first.
O(log n) complexity
The function asserts if cbm is NULL
The function reports a NULL key_pair as ccol_invalid_args, which matches
chmap_delete_elem(3)
A key_pair whose ptr is NULL and whose size is not zero promises bytes
that it has no storage for, so the function reports it as
ccol_invalid_args too
A key_pair of size zero is correct, with a ptr or without one, and
removes the empty key
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_remove (3),
.BR cbmap_insert_elem (3)
